WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.563 --> 00:00:09.297
OLIVIA: Hello, Little Fox readers, and welcome to People in the News . Today Ben has

2
00:00:09.298 --> 00:00:14.960
a cool story about a baseball player named Mo’ne Davis. She’s the American girl who

3
00:00:14.961 --> 00:00:23.365
drew worldwide attention while pitching in the 2014 Little League World Series. What’s so unusual about

4
00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:26.544
Mo'ne, Ben? BEN: First of all, Olivia, most Little League players are boys. Mo’ne was the

5
00:00:26.545 --> 00:00:31.198
18th girl to ever play in the World Series. But she was the first girl ever

6
00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:39.612
to pitch a shutout in the World Series. That means she pitched so well, the

7
00:00:39.613 --> 00:00:44.356
other team didn't even score! This was such an incredible feat that her baseball jersey is

8
00:00:44.357 --> 00:00:50.347
now in the Baseball Hall of Fame. Mo’ne Ikea Davis was born on June 24, 2001,

9
00:00:50.348 --> 00:00:57.162
in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Her stepfather is a construction worker and her mother is a nurse’s aide,

10
00:00:57.163 --> 00:01:03.263
studying to be a nurse. Mo'ne is the second of four children. The first sport

11
00:01:03.264 --> 00:01:06.157
Mo’ne played wasn’t baseball; it was basketball. When she was seven years old, Mo'ne was tossing

12
00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:10.654
around a ball with some other kids, and a coach noticed her amazing throwing ability. So

13
00:01:10.655 --> 00:01:18.566
Coach Steve Bandura asked if she’d like to join the Monarchs, a basketball team at

14
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:25.414
a local recreation center. Mo’ne was eager to join the team, but her mother was unsure.

15
00:01:25.415 --> 00:01:29.635
This was a boys’ team! Mo’ne’s mother worried that Mo'ne would get hurt or that the

16
00:01:29.636 --> 00:01:36.792
boys would be mean to her. OLIVIA: Was it hard for Mo’ne to go to her

17
00:01:36.793 --> 00:01:40.630
first practice? BEN: She felt shy, but she was excited too. She joined right in,

18
00:01:40.631 --> 00:01:49.036
and was soon playing in games and scoring lots of points. In fact, many people think

19
00:01:49.037 --> 00:01:53.968
she’s better at basketball than baseball. Every Monarch player was required to play two other sports:

20
00:01:53.969 --> 00:02:02.445
baseball and soccer. Mo’ne quickly excelled at all three. Coach Bandura taught the basics of each

21
00:02:02.446 --> 00:02:07.270
sport as well as good sportsmanship. Monarchs were expected to be gracious whether they won

22
00:02:07.271 --> 00:02:11.187
or lost. Most of all, the coach encouraged the kids to have fun. Coach Bandura also

23
00:02:11.188 --> 00:02:15.437
took an interest in Mo’ne’s education. When he realized how smart she was, he encouraged her

24
00:02:15.438 --> 00:02:19.282
to apply to one of Philadelphia’s best private schools. She became an honors student at

25
00:02:19.283 --> 00:02:26.002
the school, even though it was a very long bus ride every day and she was

26
00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:33.344
busy with sports. Some nights Mo'ne had to stay up until one in the morning to

27
00:02:33.345 --> 00:02:38.508
get her schoolwork done. When Mo’ne was ten, she almost quit baseball. She thought it was

28
00:02:38.509 --> 00:02:44.673
slow compared to basketball. She also hated the team’s demanding schedule. That summer her family

29
00:02:44.674 --> 00:02:48.002
visited five water parks; Mo’ne missed every outing! OLIVIA: What changed Mo'ne's mind? BEN: Her teammates

30
00:02:48.003 --> 00:02:53.595
didn't want her to quit, and the Monarchs were about to take a trip across the

31
00:02:53.596 --> 00:02:56.313
country to play other baseball teams. So Mo'ne kept playing. In her autobiography, Mo’ne Davis: Remember

32
00:02:56.314 --> 00:03:05.067
My Name , Mo'ne encourages other kids to stick with activities too: "Look at all

33
00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:12.807
the things I would have missed out on if I had quit baseball." Mo’ne’s schedule got

34
00:03:12.808 --> 00:03:17.049
even busier when she started playing baseball for a team called the Taney Dragons. Unlike the

35
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:23.388
Monarchs, the Dragons were an official Little League team. Mo'ne became the Dragons' best pitcher.

36
00:03:23.389 --> 00:03:27.624
She could throw a fastball that traveled about seventy miles per hour! In a playoff game

37
00:03:27.625 --> 00:03:34.717
in 2014, Mo'ne pitched so well, she shut out the other team. And the win advanced

38
00:03:34.718 --> 00:03:41.288
the Dragons to the Little League World Series! By the time the Dragons arrived at the

39
00:03:41.289 --> 00:03:45.683
World Series, everyone was talking about the Dragons and the girl with the fastball. Outside

40
00:03:45.684 --> 00:03:52.459
Philadelphia’s city hall, giant TVs were set up so people could watch the Dragons play. In

41
00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:58.513
the team's first World Series game, Mo’ne pitched another shutout. The crowd watching her in the

42
00:03:58.514 --> 00:04:03.361
stadium went wild! TV stations and newspapers wanted to interview her. Everybody wanted her autograph.

43
00:04:03.362 --> 00:04:10.009
Many famous people—sports stars, movie stars, even Mrs. Obama, the U.S. president's wife—tweeted about her accomplishment!

44
00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:20.996
Mo'ne was the youngest athlete ever to appear on the cover of Sports Illustrated magazine, which

45
00:04:20.997 --> 00:04:27.926
was a huge honor. And the next time she pitched, more than five million people worldwide

46
00:04:27.927 --> 00:04:34.169
watched on TV. Some people worried that all this attention was too much for a

47
00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:38.747
13-year-old. But Mo’ne stayed cool on the baseball field and in front of the cameras. Although

48
00:04:38.748 --> 00:04:43.148
Mo'ne liked being a role model for girls, she didn’t like the media focusing just on

49
00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:49.551
her. She insisted that baseball was a team sport, and she couldn’t have achieved her success

50
00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:55.644
without her teammates. Many people admired her even more because of this humble attitude. In

51
00:04:55.645 --> 00:05:00.388
the Dragons' fourth game, they were defeated by a team from Chicago. This team went on

52
00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:04.885
to win the U.S. championship, but lost the world championship to South Korea. That year, among

53
00:05:04.886 --> 00:05:10.637
seven thousand Little League teams in the world, Mo’ne’s team came in third. By 2015

54
00:05:10.638 --> 00:05:16.620
Mo’ne was too old to play Little League anymore, but she continued to play sports for

55
00:05:16.621 --> 00:05:23.689
her school. Her goal is to someday play women’s basketball for the University of Connecticut, a

56
00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:31.698
school that has won many national championships. Since the Little League World Series, Mo’ne has had

57
00:05:31.699 --> 00:05:36.522
many other opportunities. Disney began making a TV movie about her life, and since she

58
00:05:36.523 --> 00:05:43.601
loves sneakers she helped to design a line of them. Some of the profits from these

59
00:05:43.602 --> 00:05:49.662
sneakers go to a charity that helps impoverished girls around the world. Even though Mo'ne is

60
00:05:49.663 --> 00:05:59.101
famous now, everyone says she’s still the same Mo’ne—a girl who likes to have fun and

61
00:05:59.102 --> 00:06:05.560
is always ready to cheer up a friend. OLIVIA: Thanks, Ben. It sounds like Mo'ne

62
00:06:05.561 --> 00:06:12.373
will be a champion, no matter what she does next! See you soon, Little Fox readers!

